@lamontlauren367 On the other hand it wouldn't require swapping out stones when they could just swap out the entire piece. These pieces aren't one of a kind at these stores, and I guarantee the display pieces are the best looking ones they can find in the stock.

Jewelry is one of the biggest scams from the get go. You never wear the nice pieces BECAUSE you spend thousands for them, and it isn't like some $3000 necklace from Kay Jewelers is going to be a retirement plan because jewelry aftermarket prices are garbage, almost as bad as used cars.

Also, keep in mind, the lighting in jewelry stores is specifically designed to elicit the biggest, and brightest sparkles out of their DISPLAY pieces. Once you get most cut stones in natural lighting- or even worse your home's lighting- yeah they'll sparkle but not NEARLY as cleanly nor as brightly. If you've ever walked into a jewelry store and thought the lighting was... different, this is why. It literally is. It's actually why I hate walking into jewelers. The lighting just feels wrong to me, oppressive almost.
